---

<BaseLayout
  title="Germany B1 settlement and citizenship"
  description={description}
  canonicalURL="https://visalang.org/germany-b1-settlement-citizenship/"
  jsonLD={jsonLD}
  enableAds={true}
>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <article class="guide-article route-hub-page">
      <p class="eyebrow">Germany · B1 · settlement and citizenship</p>
      <h1>Germany B1 for settlement permits and citizenship</h1>
      <p class="guide-summary">Start with the authority and legal route that apply to your application. This hub helps you separate language proof from the wider file, compare only relevant exam products, and keep local dates and terms tied to current official sources.</p>
      <p>Family reunion is a separate procedure. Use the <a href="/germany-family-reunion-a1/">Germany A1 family-reunion hub</a> for that path. A <a href="/zh/germany-family-reunion-a1/">Chinese A1 topic</a> exists for family reunion only; this B1 hub is English.</p>

      <h2 id="quick-answer">Quick answer</h2>
      <p>Name the path before you name an exam. Settlement and citizenship are separate files. On BAMF’s settlement overview (English page date still 01/03/2024; German and English pages opened in the 2026-11-14 source set), the general settlement and EU long-term descriptions tie adequate German to B1. The protection route on that page uses A2, and a further sentence on that route uses C1. The EU Blue Card paragraphs use “basic knowledge” for one case and “sufficient understanding” for another. Those are not the same threshold. Naturalisation is a different file. The current Nationality Act section 10, opened 2026-11-14 on gesetze-im-internet, states five years of lawful ordinary residence in subsection (1). Language (no. 6) is separate from knowledge of the legal and social order (no. 7). Subsections (3) and (3a) are marked weggefallen on that current text. The Act of 27 October 2025 (BGBl. 2025 I No. 256) deletes section 10 subsection 3; it does not itself delete subsection 3a. BAMF’s German naturalisation overview dated 9 November 2026 lists five years, B1, and the naturalisation test. BAMF’s English naturalisation page still dated 30 June 2025 still describes a three-year special-integration possibility. That English sentence conflicts with the statute and the German overview. Treat it as a historical conflict, not as a route to choose. This hub does not say that every three-year path in nationality law has ended. Section 9 and discretionary naturalisation are separate questions for the nationality authority. A language result does not prove civic knowledge, and Leben in Deutschland does not prove B1.</p>

      <div class="guide-trustbar" aria-label="Verification boundary">
        <span>The competent authority decides your route, evidence and outcome.</span>
        <span>VisaLang does not decide eligibility, exemptions, certificate acceptance or application results.</span>
      </div>

      <ArticleTOC headings={headings} />

      <h2 id="who-decides">Who decides and what to confirm first</h2>
      <p>For a settlement-permit question, identify the statutory route and the competent local foreigners authority. For naturalisation, use the competent nationality authority. <a href="https://bamf-navi.bamf.de/en/Themen/Behoerden/?typ=ABH">BAMF-NAvI</a> helps you find an office. It does not publish a national list of accepted B1 or DTZ formats. Germany has different settlement routes, and their language thresholds are not identical. “Sufficient German” on one path is not the label on the next path. Do not convert a general B1 statement into a conclusion about your case.</p>
      <p>Save the authority's current page, portal, form or written reply, the route it names, and the date checked. An exam provider can define its product, but it cannot decide whether that proof fits your application.</p>

      <h2 id="decision-order">Decision order for settlement vs citizenship</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Identify whether you are checking a <strong>settlement-permit</strong> procedure, a <strong>naturalisation</strong> procedure, or both as separate files.</li>
        <li>Locate the competent authority for that procedure (foreigners authority vs nationality authority).</li>
        <li>Confirm the statutory route or application category the authority names.</li>
        <li>Separate language proof from civic-knowledge evidence and from every other condition.</li>
        <li>Ask which language documents are accepted and in what form.</li>
        <li>Compare only named exam products, then verify a centre, timeline, and submission recheck.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Do not apply one universal “B1 rule” across settlement and citizenship. Keep two folders if you are pursuing both.</p>

      <h2 id="responsibility-split">Responsibility split</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Who decides</th><th scope="col">Start here</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Settlement route and full eligibility</th><td>Competent local foreigners authority</td><td><a href="/guides/goethe-b1-germany-settlement-work/">Settlement permit and B1</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Naturalisation route and full file</th><td>Competent nationality authority</td><td><a href="/guides/germany-b1-citizenship-language-proof/">Citizenship language proof</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Civic-knowledge evidence</th><td>Same nationality authority (when required)</td><td><a href="/guides/germany-b1-leben-in-deutschland-and-language-proof/">Language and civic knowledge</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Exam product execution</th><td>Exam owner + authorised centre</td><td><a href="/guides/goethe-b1-vs-telc-b1/">DTZ / Goethe / telc comparison</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="route-entry">Choose the correct route entry</h2>
      <div class="guide-related-grid">
        <a class="article-card" href="/guides/goethe-b1-germany-settlement-work/"><p class="card-category">Settlement permit</p><h3>Identify the route before treating B1 as relevant</h3><p>Separate the language question from all other permit conditions.</p></a>
        <a class="article-card" href="/guides/germany-b1-citizenship-language-proof/"><p class="card-category">Naturalisation</p><h3>Keep B1 language evidence inside the wider file</h3><p>Confirm the current evidence and local procedure with the nationality authority.</p></a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="separate-evidence">Keep language and civic knowledge separate</h2>
      <p>B1 language evidence and civic-knowledge evidence answer different questions. BAMF’s integration-course examination page describes the Deutsch-Test für Zuwanderer as the language test and Leben in Deutschland as the orientation test. The orientation-course pass on that page is at least 15 of 33. Proof of the naturalisation knowledge condition follows the naturalisation-test ordinance at at least 17 of 33. The <a href="/guides/germany-b1-leben-in-deutschland-and-language-proof/">Leben in Deutschland guide</a> keeps those figures with their purposes. Do not swap them, and do not treat either figure as a B1 score.</p>
      <p>Do not read the settlement page as “everyone needs B1”. Use the <a href="/guides/goethe-b1-germany-settlement-work/">settlement guide</a> for Residence Act sections 9 and 18c, then ask the foreigners authority which sentence applies. Use the <a href="/guides/germany-b1-citizenship-language-proof/">citizenship guide</a> for section 10 as it stands now, including the conflict with the outdated English overview. Compare DTZ, Goethe B1, and telc B1 only after the authority has named a language document. Goethe-Zertifikat B1 product HTML was not reopened for this correction. The B1 administration rules (Stand 1 September 2025) describe modules. They do not decide acceptance.</p>

      <h2 id="compare-proof">Compare only possible proof</h2>
      <p>After the authority identifies possible language evidence, use the <a href="/guides/goethe-b1-vs-telc-b1/">DTZ, Goethe B1 and telc B1 comparison</a>. Compare exact products and document formats, not general recognition claims, anecdotes or an exam brand in isolation.</p>

      <h2 id="verify-centre">Verify the selected centre</h2>
      <p>Use the <a href="/guides/goethe-b1-fees-and-booking/">fees and booking guide</a> to record the selected official or authorised centre, exact product, date, seat, fee scope, identification, cancellation, accessibility, result and certificate process. These local details remain time-sensitive even when this page's source review is current.</p>

      <h2 id="build-timeline">Build the timeline from confirmed dates</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/guides/germany-b1-settlement-citizenship-timeline/">B1 evidence timeline</a> starts from dates supplied by you, the competent authority or the selected centre. It does not calculate residence periods, processing time or eligibility, and it does not insert a default retake or appointment buffer.</p>

      <h2 id="submission-recheck">Finish with a submission recheck</h2>
      <p>Use the <a href="/guides/germany-b1-settlement-citizenship-checklist/">settlement and citizenship checklist</a> as the terminal route step. Compare every note with the authority's current instructions, resolve mismatches, and leave unresolved items open rather than forcing the route back into a loop.</p>

      <h2 id="pitfalls">Route-level pitfalls</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Choosing Goethe or telc before the authority has named the path and the document.</li>
        <li>Treating a B1 exam booking as proof that the whole settlement or citizenship file is ready.</li>
        <li>Planning section 10 residence from BAMF’s English naturalisation page dated 30 June 2025, including its special-integration three-year sentence.</li>
        <li>Merging settlement-permit answers into a naturalisation folder without authority confirmation.</li>
        <li>Treating language proof and Leben in Deutschland / civic-knowledge evidence as the same item.</li>
        <li>Ranking Goethe vs telc by brand before the authority names acceptable proof.</li>
        <li>Entering default retake or processing buffers the centre or authority never confirmed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="route-record">Minimum route record</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Entry</th><th scope="col">Source + date</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Procedure (settlement / citizenship / both)</th><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Competent authority</th><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Route / provision named</th><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Language proof wording</th><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Civic-knowledge wording (if any)</th><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Stop rule before exam payment</th><td></td><td></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="preparation-support">Optional preparation support</h2>
      <p>Preparation becomes useful only after the exam product is relevant and clear. Start with the <a href="/guides/goethe-b1-study-plan/">adjustable study plan</a>, which now holds the module self-check. Then open only the skill page that matches a gap you recorded:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/guides/goethe-b1-listening-deep-dive/">Listening practice</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/goethe-b1-mock-exam-routine/">Mock-exam routine</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/goethe-b1-speaking-topics/">Speaking practice</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/goethe-b1-writing-assessment/">Writing self-check</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="tool-boundary">Planning tools and their limits</h2>
      <p><a href="/tools/route-finder/">Route Finder</a> can help frame an authority question. The <a href="/tools/exam-comparison/">Exam Comparison</a>, <a href="/tools/timeline-calculator/">Timeline calculator</a> and <a href="/tools/checklist-generator/">Checklist generator</a> organise information you enter; they do not verify acceptance, dates or eligibility.</p>
      <p>Browse the <a href="/guides/category/germany-b1/">Germany B1 guide collection</a>. <a href="/route-review/">Route Review</a> is not accepting submissions and is outside primary navigation. It does not decide a file.</p>

      <section class="official-sources">
        <h2 id="official-sources">Official starting points</h2>
        <p>Statutes and the German naturalisation overview were read from the 2026-11-14 source snapshots for the points named below. They are not a local document list and not a personal outcome. The English naturalisation page is listed only as a conflicting historical page.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.bamf.de/EN/Themen/MigrationAufenthalt/ZuwandererDrittstaaten/Migrathek/Niederlassen/niederlassen-node.html">BAMF: Settling in Germany</a> — English page date 01/03/2024. Language labels differ by route.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/aufenthg_2004/__9.html">Residence Act, Section 9</a> — in the general case, no. 7 language and no. 8 basic legal and social knowledge are separate. Not proof that section 9 is your route.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/stag/__10.html">Nationality Act, Section 10</a> — subsection (1) five years; (3) and (3a) weggefallen on the current text; no. 6 and no. 7 stay separate. Not every naturalisation path.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.recht.bund.de/bgbl/1/2025/256/regelungstext.pdf?__blob=publicationFile">BGBl. 2025 I No. 256</a> — Article 1 deletes section 10 subsection 3. Article 6 (1) brings that Act into force the day after promulgation, except Article 5. Do not attribute the repeal of subsection 3a to this announcement.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.bamf.de/DE/Themen/Integration/ZugewanderteTeilnehmende/Einbuergerung/einbuergerung-node.html">BAMF: Einbürgerung (German)</a> — Datum 09.11.2026. Five years, B1, and the naturalisation test. Not a case decision.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.bamf.de/EN/Themen/Integration/ZugewanderteTeilnehmende/Einbuergerung/einbuergerung-node.html">BAMF: Naturalisation in Germany (English)</a> — Date 30/06/2025. Historical conflict only. Do not choose its “after just three years” sentence.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.bamf.de/EN/Themen/Integration/ZugewanderteTeilnehmende/Integrationskurse/Abschlusspruefung/abschlusspruefung-node.html">BAMF: the final examination</a> — DTZ and Leben in Deutschland. The residence sentences on the English page are not the current rule.</li>
          <li><a href="https://bamf-navi.bamf.de/en/Themen/Behoerden/?typ=ABH">BAMF-NAvI authority locator</a> — find the office. Not an acceptance table.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.einbuergerung.de/ablauf.php?l=en">Federal naturalisation procedure</a> — step order. Fee and duration figures are not copied here.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.telc.net/en/language-examinations/certificate-exams/german/certificate-german-telc-german-b1/">telc Deutsch B1</a> — product page, not acceptance.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <p class="compliance-line">Planning information only. VisaLang does not give legal or immigration advice and does not decide a settlement permit, citizenship, an exemption, or an exam result. Verify the route with the competent authority and the selected centre before acting.</p>
      <p class="last-updated">Section 10, BGBl. 2025 I No. 256, and the German BAMF naturalisation overview were read from the 2026-11-14 snapshots. Goethe-Zertifikat B1 product HTML was not reopened. This page does not state fees, processing times, or individual outcomes. This B1 hub has no Chinese mirror. Not deployed.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
